  "verbalization": "Infliximab, a tumor necrosis factor (TNF) inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthropathies through its role in modulating inflammatory responses. The evidence suggests that Infliximab may be associated with an increased susceptibility to spondyloarthropathy, as it is indicated for Ankylosing Spondylitis and Rheumatoid Arthritis, both of which are classified under Spondyloarthropathy, Susceptibility To. This relationship is supported by the indirect pathways through which Infliximab influences disease susceptibility, particularly via its effect on TNF signaling, which is a key driver of inflammation in these conditions. The presence of multiple indirect pathways, including the involvement of Golimumab and Ixekizumab, further underscores the potential for Infliximab to contribute to the development of spondyloarthropathy through a shared mechanism of action. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of TNF inhibition in the pathophysiology of spondyloarthropathy, suggesting that Infliximab may have a role in modulating disease susceptibility through its impact on inflammatory processes.",
